\f1\fs22 The 
\f2\i Stickett Inn
\f1\i0  was an open club, allowing entry to all and sundry, 
\f2\i if 
\f1\i0 Master All or Mistress Sundry met the dress regulations. Tony had no illusions about his dress sense; he was irretrievably in the deep end of the sundry pool and he never wore the correct bathing suit. But Tony knew the bouncers at the 
\f2\i Stickett
\f1\i0  (all corrupt ex-cops) and could slip in with a wink and a nod.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>His mates were already there, not at their regular table, but a table on the other side of the club, still near enough the dance floor for gawking, and far enough for conversation. Halo was already scanning the dancers like a sniper. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Found her yet?\'92 Tony asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo did not acknowledge his presence. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91He\'92s already in search-and-destroy mode,\'92 Major said. Tony rolled his eyes and nodded. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91C\'92mon Halo,\'92 Abe said. \'91Tony\'92s here.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Can\'92t talk. Hunting,\'92 Halo said in psycho-killer monotone. The others laughed. Halo was a riot.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Say hello, Halo,\'92 pleaded Abe. \'91Think of Tony. He\'92s single again. He needs cheering up.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Andrea is a fake Stacy bitch,\'92 Halo said. \'91She deserves to die.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In spite of the crowd, the music, and the gaming machines, silence descended around their table.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo broke his trance and lightened up. \'91Too much?\'92 He grinned and shone his lighthouse eyes upon them. With relief, the others joined him in his sudden humour. \'91So, I hope you know,\'92 he said to Tony, \'91your friends are looking after you. Never be sad.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Andrea doesn\'92t matter,\'92 Tony said. \'91I met the perfect woman today.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Two o\'92clock!\'92 Halo said, and scrambled to the dance floor.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There he goes,\'92 Major said with dismay. \'91He\'92ll score again for sure.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What\'92s he got that I haven\'92t?\'92 Abe challenged.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony and Major let the awkward silence swell, and even refrained from sipping their frothy brews.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Halo had a technique, but he\'92d never tell. \'91I know the shortcut to a woman\'92s heart,\'92 was his complete and repetitious explanation. Whatever it was, Halo and his 
\f2\i cible d'amour 
\f1\i0 would usually be out of the club so fast, she would leave coat and handbag. However, this time, Halo\'92s technique failed him, and he returned to the table, sullen and doubly determined. The others knew to leave him to his ruminations and move the conversation elsewhere.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the boys were discussing the form of last year\'92s premiership winners, Halo brightened, and\'97mouth agape with wonder, and pupils dilated\'97his engorgement discovered his underwear needed French windows. \'91Oh, tax,\'92 he cursed. \'91It\'92s her. The one.\'92
